# Rust patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Rust design and its common anti-patterns. Applies only to `*.rs` files.

## Crate and module layout

- `src/lib.rs` (or `src/main.rs` for a binary) is the crate root; group
  related functionality into modules under `src/`, each with a clear
  single responsibility — a module that only holds shared types with no
  behavior ("models", "types", "common") invites the same cross-module
  coupling a Go "utils" package does.
- A workspace (`[workspace]` in the root `Cargo.toml`) splits genuinely
  independent crates (a library plus a CLI that depends on it, or several
  services sharing a core crate) — do not default to a workspace for a
  single-binary project that has no reason to publish separate crates.
- Keep `pub` surface intentional: re-export the types callers need from
  `lib.rs`, keep internal modules `pub(crate)` or private so the compiler
  (not convention) enforces the boundary.

## Error handling design

- A library crate defines its own error enum with `thiserror`, one
  variant per failure mode, `#[source]`/`#[from]` on any variant that
  wraps another error so `source()` and `?`-based conversion both work —
  never collapse distinct failure modes into a single `Other(String)`
  variant that erases the caller's ability to match on what happened.
- An application/binary crate uses `anyhow::Result` at its outer layers
  and `.context("doing x")`/`.with_context(|| ...)` at each call to `?`
  that crosses a meaningful boundary, so a failure's final error chain
  reads as a story, not a single opaque message.
- Never use a `Result<T, String>`/`Result<T, Box<dyn Error>>` as a
  permanent design in a library — `Box<dyn Error>` is a reasonable
  quick-prototype return type but forces every caller to string-match or
  downcast; graduate to a concrete `thiserror` enum once the error shapes
  stabilize.

## Ownership-driven API design

- Take `&self`/`&mut self`/`self` deliberately: `self` (by value) only
  when the method consumes or transforms the value into something else
  (a builder's terminal `.build()`), `&mut self` when it mutates in place,
  `&self` otherwise.
- The typestate/builder pattern (a `FooBuilder` with chained `with_x`
  methods returning `Self`, a terminal `.build() -> Result<Foo, ...>`) for
  a constructor with many optional parameters, instead of a boolean-heavy
  positional signature.
- Prefer newtypes (`struct UserId(u64);`) over a bare primitive when a
  value has domain meaning distinct from its representation — the
  compiler then rejects passing a `ProductId` where a `UserId` is
  expected, which a bare `u64` parameter cannot.

## Trait design

- Define a trait at the point of abstraction a consumer actually needs
  (an `impl Trait` argument or a generic bound), sized to the methods that
  consumer calls — a trait with every method the concrete type happens to
  have is not an abstraction, it is a restatement of the type.
- Prefer generics (`fn process<T: Processor>(p: &T)`) for a hot path where
  monomorphization's code-size cost is acceptable and static dispatch
  matters; use `Box<dyn Trait>`/`&dyn Trait` when you need a heterogeneous
  collection or a boundary (a plugin registry, a callback list) where the
  concrete type varies at runtime.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A `.clone()` inserted to make the borrow checker pass without
  considering whether restructuring ownership or borrowing differently
  would avoid the copy — flag any clone in a hot path with no comment
  explaining why it is necessary.
- Stringly-typed errors (`Err(format!("something went wrong: {e}"))`
  returned as `Result<T, String>`) in a library crate instead of a
  `thiserror`-derived enum a caller can match on.
- A "god module" (`utils`, `helpers`, `common`) accumulating unrelated
  free functions — split by what the code actually does, the same
  anti-pattern flagged in Go/Python's own pattern rules.
- `unwrap()`/`expect()` reached for as a shortcut past a type error during
  development and never revisited before the change ships.
